Q: What is the point of a default argument set if the variables can&#39;t be used? A: <p>If you want <code>MyClass</code> to have a member named <code>x</code> you need to define that. The parameter to its constructor is local to the constructor, just like any other function parameter.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;

class MyClass {
public:
    // this initializer list may look confusing (initializing x with x),
    // but the x being initialized is the member, and the value used to 
    // initilize it is the parameter.
    MyClass(int x = 123) : x(x) {
    }

    int x;
};

int main() {
    MyClass myObject;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; myObject.x; //no longer an error
}
</code></pre>
<p>I'd note, however, that many people tend to frown upon classes having public data members.</p>

Q: how to use Promise with express in node.js? A: <pre><code>router.post('/Registration', function(req, res, next) {
    return User
        .findOne({ username: req.body.username })
        .then((user) =&gt; {
            if (user) {
                return { msg:&quot;Username already been taken&quot; };
            }
            return { msg: &quot;Username available.&quot; };
        })
        .catch((err)=&gt;{
            next(err);
        });
});
</code></pre>
<p>You can write a clean code like this.
Promise is a global variable available, you don't need to require it.</p>
